  "hypothesis": "Does native spatial QSM use information beyond normalized singular values, and does finer resolution help?",
  "geometry": "Columns are overlapping native patches H[a,b]=X[a+b]; windows floor(shape/2). Raster is a matched alternative lift.",
  "normalization": "Each original example z-scored BEFORE fixed nonoverlapping spatial mean convolution; QSM H/Frobenius norm; amplitude L2 norm.",
  "qsm": "Four fixed-time uploads, t=2, |0> initial state, existing SU(4) brickwall mixers and local Z + linear readout; zero Hamiltonian padding.",
  "matched_qsm": "Same qubit register, initialization, optimizer and readout across native/raster/spectrum arms at each resolution.",
  "spectrum_control": "Keep normalized block eigenvalues, replace eigenvectors by fixed Walsh-Hadamard basis; removes input-dependent subspaces.",
  "classical_probe": "StandardScaler + RBF SVC, C=1, 11 gamma candidates logspace(-3,3)/dimension; validation chooses gamma. Singular values rounded to 10 decimals.",
  "learned_feature_probe": "Fixed C=1 standardized logistic regression on initial vs trained local-Z features; round to 10 decimals; no test tuning.",
  "splits": "Real: 180 fit / 60 val / 120 test, balanced official train/test subsets. Synthetic: 120 fit / 40 val / 160 test, complete twin pairs within splits.",
  "selection": "Validation balanced accuracy; exact ties by qubits, depth, candidate name; no refit, no epoch selection.",
  "limitations": "Known benchmark pilot, finite ideal simulation, fixed upload time and patch size; no hardware efficiency or universal encoding claim. Cross-family readouts differ.",
